# LIVE REGRESSION FIXTURE — the trait-object pack at the SET consuming
# positions, and the ONE-ARGUMENT shape of the key-value writer family.
#
# ⭐ THE DISCRIMINATOR — WHY THIS FILE EXISTS SEPARATELY FROM THE `Vector` AND
# `Dict` ROWS. `Set.insert` is a ONE-ARG ALIAS for `Set.add` on the same
# runtime callee (regenerate:
#   grep -n 'name: "insert"' src/ir/lowering/builtins.rs
# → the SET arm, `runtime_callee: Some("gorget_set_add")`, `params: elem_param`).
# Every OTHER member of the `put`/`set`/`insert`/`fill`/`get_or_put` family
# takes TWO arguments, so the value-slot index was originally computed by a
# match arm guarded `args.len() >= 2`. A one-arg `insert` failed that guard and
# fell through to "this method has no value slot" — while the destination TYPE
# for it was computed correctly three lines above, in a match arm that lists
# `"insert"` by name. The hint was computed and then thrown away, and
# `Set[Box[Trait]].insert(temp)` stayed a live stack-buffer-overflow (bare
# rc 135, SIGBUS) while `.add(temp)` on the identical receiver was already
# fixed. Index and type now come out of ONE match, so they cannot disagree.
#
# ⚠ ONE ROW PER SPELLING × ONE ROW PER PROTOCOL (Core #4 / Core #12).
# `add` and `insert` are two spellings of one consuming position; `Set` and
# `HashSet` are two protocols reaching it, and `HashSet`'s method table IS
# `SET.methods` held BY REFERENCE, so no text-derived census of
# `BuiltinMethodDecl` rows can see it. The `add` rows are here because the
# fix that closed them shipped with no regression net of its own — a fix
# nothing pins is next round's regression.
#
# ⚠ ONE ELEMENT PER SET, DELIBERATELY. A `Set[Box[Trait]]` hashes the
# `{data, vtable}` PAIR — pointer identity — so two structurally equal
# `Robot`s occupy two slots and iteration order over more than one element is
# not a property this fixture may assert. Whether `Set[Box[Trait]]` should be
# accepted at all is an open owner question; this file pins only that the
# accepted form is MEMORY-SAFE and dispatches correctly, and takes no position
# on the accept/reject axis.
#
# ⚠ THE INSERTED VALUE MUST BE A TEMP. A `Box` is single-owner-by-design, so a
# live named source at these positions is `E_MoveWithoutOperator` at check
# time; the temp form is the only shape that reaches the pack here.
#
# ⚠ Payloads are heap-forced through `mk`. A literal would not reallocate and
# would hide a stale-pointer read.
#
# ⚠ THE REST OF THE ONE-ARGUMENT FAMILY IS PINNED ELSEWHERE, NOT IMPLIED HERE.
# `GUARD.set` and `WRITE_GUARD.set` are the other two one-argument members
# (`params: elem_param`, same grep as above); both were rc 135 for the same
# reason and are covered by `box_trait_pack_guard_set.gg`. `Shared[Mutex[…]]`
# is NOT covered by either file: `Shared[Mutex[Box[Speaker]]].lock()` is
# `E_NoMethodFound` on `Shared[Mutex[trait Speaker]]` — the Box is already gone
# from the printed type, which is `todo/t1082`, not this axis.
#
# ⚠ It stays in `known_gaps/` deliberately: moving the file registers a
# non-MATCH against `RUNTIME_DIFF_NONMATCH_CEILING`, and Core #9 ⊕ forbids
# raising that ceiling for a round's own inflow. The self-host lane has no
# analogue of the GIR-level pack at consuming positions (`todo/t1084`).
#
# INTENDED: prints `A1!`, `S2!`, `B3!`, `H4!` on both backends.
from std.collections import Box
from std.collections import Set
from std.collections import HashSet

trait Speaker:
    String speak(self)

struct Robot:
    String id

equip Robot with Speaker:
    String speak(self):
        return self.id + "!"

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

void main():
    Set[Box[Speaker]] a = Set[Box[Speaker]]()
    a.add(Box.new(Robot(mk("A", "1"))))
    for e in a:
        print(e.speak())

    Set[Box[Speaker]] s = Set[Box[Speaker]]()
    s.insert(Box.new(Robot(mk("S", "2"))))
    for e in s:
        print(e.speak())

    HashSet[Box[Speaker]] b = HashSet[Box[Speaker]]()
    b.add(Box.new(Robot(mk("B", "3"))))
    for e in b:
        print(e.speak())

    HashSet[Box[Speaker]] h = HashSet[Box[Speaker]]()
    h.insert(Box.new(Robot(mk("H", "4"))))
    for e in h:
        print(e.speak())
